  item Bake a tart in 10 minutes, no oven: quickbake-deals.example
  button Lemon tart for a crowd (bakers-bench.example) -> ad-click
  button Mini lemon tarts (partybakes.example) -> ad-click
mail mail-dessert
  from Priya Shah <priya.shah@northwind.example>
  subject Last one: the bake-off recipe
  link search-dessert
  | Dana,
  |
  | Forget the video. This search should find the same tart. Pick one and send me the page.
  |
  | Priya
notify mail Priya Shah
  | Last one: the bake-off recipe

step Not the page from the morning
  look the search results: compare them with the lunch search
  when visit search-dessert
  move Click the first result, the lemon tart.
  | The lunch search had two plain results. This one has a block of adverts above them that the lunch search did not have.
open search-dessert

step A click that took a detour
  look the address bar: it is not the recipe site
  when choose search-dessert any
  move Click “Continue to the recipe” to carry on.
  | You clicked a recipe, but the page is an advertiser's, at adrouter.invalid. Flashback sends each click on a search result through an advertising address first. The advertiser pays the attackers for the click.
open ad-click

step 5 of 5, or so Dana thinks
  look the counter in the guide: the day's jobs are done
  when choose ad-click 1
  | The recipe opens and the page looks right. Nothing on the screen says anything is wrong. Press Next to see what Flashback has done.
open lemon-tart
quota +1

step What Flashback did
  look the animation: one {PC|Mac} in a crowd of 600,000
  | Every search click on this {PC|Mac} now earns the attackers money. It is one of more than 600,000 that Flashback joined into one network.
  | Later versions needed no installer at all: a hacked page and an out-of-date Java were enough. The fix is an update, in {Windows Update|Software Update}.
attack spyware-surveillance
  | search clicks sold to advertisers
  | one machine joins a network of 600,000
  | the owner sees nothing wrong
block 203.0.113.40 80
open updates

flag hard section recipes 1
  | A recipe page that asks you to install a plug-in before its video plays, and offers the installer itself. The caterer's site asked for nothing like it.
flag hard file Downloads/Install Flash Player.pkg
  | An installer offered by a recipe page, not by the plug-in's maker.
flag hard dialog open-warning flashback
  | The warning says {Unknown Publisher|the developer is unidentified}: a real installer is signed, so it names its maker.
flag hard dialog elevation flashback
  | An installer a web page offered, from an unknown developer, asking for full control of the computer.
flag hard startup com.java.update.plist
  | A “Java update” that starts at every login, installed by a Flash installer. Flashback added it.
flag hard page ad-click
  | A click on a search result led to an advertiser's address first. This is how Flashback was paid.
flag soft file Desktop/harbor-login.txt
  | A password in a text file on the desktop: any program that runs can read it. Use a password manager.

lesson
  | A web page that asks you to install a plug-in is not the plug-in's maker. Get plug-ins and updates from the maker, through {Windows Update|Software Update} or its own site, never from a page that asks.
  | Flash is retired and no real page needs it. An installer a web page offered, from an unknown developer, that asks for administrator permission is a warning sign.
  | Keep the computer, the browser and Java up to date, or remove Java if you do not need it. If search results change, or a click goes through another address, check the start-up list in {Startup apps|Login Items}.
